          {
            "id": 2897169,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "In this series of lectures about the history of weather forecasting, I'll start by examining its early history. That'll be the subject of today's talk. Okay. So we'll start by going back thousands of years. Most ancient cultures had weather gods, and weather catastrophes, such as floods, played an important role in many creation myths.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2897170,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "In this series of lectures about the history of weather forecasting, I'll start by examining its early history.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 88.96,
                  "from": 80.405,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897189,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "That'll be the subject of today's talk.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 92.079994,
                  "from": 89.36,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897197,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Okay.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 93.68,
                  "from": 92.96,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897199,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "So we'll start by going back thousands of years.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 97.119995,
                  "from": 93.759995,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897209,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Most ancient cultures had weather gods, and weather catastrophes, such as floods, played an important role in many creation myths.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 110.065,
                  "from": 98.465,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2897230,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "Generally, weather was attributed to the whims of the gods, as the wide range of weather gods in various cultures shows. For instance, there's the Egyptian sun god Ra and Thor, the Norse god of thunder and lightning. Many ancient civilizations developed rites such as dances in order to make the weather gods look kindly on them. But the weather was of daily importance. Observing the skies and drawing the correct conclusions from these observations was really important.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2897231,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Generally, weather was attributed to the whims of the gods, as the wide range of weather gods in various cultures shows.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 120.55,
                  "from": 110.87,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897253,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "For instance, there's the Egyptian sun god Ra and Thor, the Norse god of thunder and lightning.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 129.535,
                  "from": 121.11,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897271,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Many ancient civilizations developed rites such as dances in order to make the weather gods look kindly on them.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 139.695,
                  "from": 130.495,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897291,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "But the weather was of daily importance.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 143.99,
                  "from": 140.735,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897299,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Observing the skies and drawing the correct conclusions from these observations was really important.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 151.99,
                  "from": 144.55,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2897314,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "In fact, their survival depended on it. It isn't known when people first started to observe the skies, but at around June, the Babylonians produced the first short range weather forecasts based on their observations of clouds and other phenomena. The Chinese also recognized weather patterns, and by March, astronomers had developed a calendar which divided the year into 24 festivals, each associated with a different weather phenomenon. The ancient Greeks were the first to develop a more scientific approach to explaining the weather. The work of the philosopher and scientist Aristotle in the fourth century BC is especially noteworthy as his ideas held sway for nearly two thousand years.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2897315,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "In fact, their survival depended on it.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 155.51001,
                  "from": 152.71,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897323,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "It isn't known when people first started to observe the skies, but at around June, the Babylonians produced the first short range weather forecasts based on their observations of clouds and other phenomena.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 175.75,
                  "from": 156.64499,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897357,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The Chinese also recognized weather patterns, and by March, astronomers had developed a calendar which divided the year into 24 festivals, each associated with a different weather phenomenon.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 193.60501,
                  "from": 176.70999,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897386,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The ancient Greeks were the first to develop a more scientific approach to explaining the weather.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 201.38,
                  "from": 194.72499,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897403,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The work of the philosopher and scientist Aristotle in the fourth century BC is especially noteworthy as his ideas held sway for nearly two thousand years.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 215.16501,
                  "from": 202.18001,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2897430,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "In March, he wrote a book in which he attempted to account for the formation of rain, clouds, wind, and storms. He also described celestial phenomena such as halos that is, bright circles of light around the sun, the moon, and bright stars and comets. Many of his observations were surprisingly accurate. For example, he believed that heat could cause water to evaporate, but he also jumped to quite a few wrong conclusions, such as that winds are breathed out by the earth. Errors like this were rectified from the Renaissance onwards.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2897431,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "In March, he wrote a book in which he attempted to account for the formation of rain, clouds, wind, and storms.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 227.61,
                  "from": 216.36499,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897453,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "He also described celestial phenomena such as halos that is, bright circles of light around the sun, the moon, and bright stars and comets.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 240.59502,
                  "from": 228.25,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897475,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Many of his observations were surprisingly accurate.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 245.39502,
                  "from": 241.475,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897483,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "For example, he believed that heat could cause water to evaporate, but he also jumped to quite a few wrong conclusions, such as that winds are breathed out by the earth.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 260.47,
                  "from": 246.035,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897515,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Errors like this were rectified from the Renaissance onwards.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 265.11002,
                  "from": 261.03,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2897525,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "For nearly two thousand years, Aristotle's work was accepted as the chief authority on weather theory. Alongside this, though, in the Middle Ages, weather observations were passed on in the form of proverbs, such as red sky at night, shepherd's delight, red sky in the morning, shepherd's warning. Many of these are based on very good observations and are accurate, as contemporary meteorologists have discovered. For centuries, any attempt to forecast the weather could only be based on personal observations. But in the fifteenth century, scientists began to see the need for instruments.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2897526,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "For nearly two thousand years, Aristotle's work was accepted as the chief authority on weather theory.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 277.845,
                  "from": 270.405,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2897543,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Alongside this, though, in the Middle Ages, weather observations were passed on in the form of proverbs, such as red sky at night, shepherd's delight, red sky in the morning, shepherd's warning.",
                "meta": {
